WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
A capstone is an argument, not a feature list.

2
00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:06.659
Every studio project so far came with a problem already chosen.

3
00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:09.733
The capstone doesn’t, and that is the hardest part.

4
00:00:10.013 --> 00:00:26.995
Most weak capstones fail in week 1, not week 5: they pick a technology (“I want to build a multi-agent system with MCP”) and then look for a user, or they pick a problem so broad (“help instructional designers”) that no amount of testing could show it works.

5
00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:39.307
Think of the capstone as an argument you will make to a skeptical reviewer: for this user, doing this job, my system beats the honest alternative by this much, at this cost, with these known risks.

6
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:45.261
Every scoping decision this week either makes that argument possible or makes it impossible.

7
00:00:45.541 --> 00:00:47.888
You can’t argue “beats” without a baseline.

8
00:00:48.168 --> 00:00:51.773
You can’t argue “by this much” without an eval set and metrics.

9
00:00:52.053 --> 00:00:55.083
You can’t argue “known risks” without having looked for them.

10
00:00:55.363 --> 00:01:03.917
This lesson walks through the five decisions that set up the argument: the problem, the metrics, the baseline, the eval set, and the risk register.

11
00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:08.891
None of them needs code, and all of them should be finished before you write any system code.

12
00:01:09.171 --> 00:01:10.621
That ordering feels slow.

13
00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:17.365
It is the reason the remaining five weeks go fast, because every later decision (“should I add hybrid search?

14
00:01:17.645 --> 00:01:19.395
”, “is the cheaper model good enough?

15
00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:22.043
”) becomes a measurement instead of a debate.
